    <statement><p>Find the derivative of the function <m>f(z)</m>, below.</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle f(z) = \frac{1}{\ln(3z)}</m></p>
<p><m>f'(z) =</m> <fillin characters="65" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{-1\cdot 1\mathopen{}\left(\ln\mathopen{}\left(3z\right)\right)^{-2}}{z}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>\displaystyle \frac{1}{\ln(3z)} = 1 (\ln(3z))^{-1}</m>, so
that, applying the chain rule,

<m>\displaystyle f'(z) = -1 (\ln(3 z))^{-2}\cdot\left(\frac{3}{3 z}\right) = 
    \frac{-1}{z (\ln(3z))^2}</m>



</solution>
